# The modular form attached to an elliptic curve is a normalized eigenform

Theorem statement: `HorizontalPadicL.ellipticCurve_attachedForm_isNormalizedEigenform`
(`ed384a33-14c3-4820-90c0-420e322533f1`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-17.

Proof: submission `52f22168-7e7f-43df-a87f-2632021b6e7b`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-18 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

For a modular elliptic curve over the rationals, the chosen least-level weight-two cusp form whose
q-expansion equals the elliptic curve L-series satisfies the normalized eigenform coefficient
relations: normalization, coprime multiplicativity, and the good- and bad-prime power recurrences.

## Explanation of the source proof

The elliptic-curve Euler product first gives normalization and multiplicativity. Each local power
series has constant coefficient one and prime-power residue-field cardinality, so Mathlib's
Euler-product multiplicativity theorem shows
$$
a_1(E)=1,\qquad a_{mn}(E)=a_m(E)a_n(E)\quad ((m,n)=1).
$$
The imported conductor-support theorem supplies the remaining local information. At a prime outside
the least modular level it gives
$$
a_{p^{r+2}}=a_p a_{p^{r+1}}-p a_{p^r},
$$
while at a prime dividing the level it gives
$$
a_{p^{r+2}}=a_p a_{p^{r+1}}.
$$
Finally, the defining coefficient equality for the chosen attached cusp form transports all four
identities from the elliptic-curve $L$-series to its q-expansion. These are exactly the fields of
`CuspForm.IsNormalizedEigenform`.

